         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "3f90fa48-35a6-4a96-ad0a-8f1ed8c17909",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "thinking",
         "source_menu": [],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Let Us Think!\n\nIs there anything around us that is truly flat -- something with only two dimensions?",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "634a405f-b53c-49c1-b7ce-3d6d6a8e8d13",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": "no",
         "score": 2,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "634a405f-b53c-49c1-b7ce-3d6d6a8e8d13",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "happy",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "c137d968-eb5a-47e7-90b1-1c4cadd47ca9",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=c137d968-eb5a-47e7-90b1-1c4cadd47ca9",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Everything Has Three Dimensions\n\nEvery object, person, animal, and plant around us has three dimensions: length, height, and depth. Because they occupy space in three dimensions, they have mass.\n\nThis has been true for billions of years. The idea of a plane -- or of a single dimension -- is much more recent: it is a simplification our minds created, not something nature shows us.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "ed96cef3-22ff-4c4d-910c-ec16e8e0a3b1",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "ed96cef3-22ff-4c4d-910c-ec16e8e0a3b1",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "party",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "c137d968-eb5a-47e7-90b1-1c4cadd47ca9",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=c137d968-eb5a-47e7-90b1-1c4cadd47ca9",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Conclusion\n\nIn this class we learned that even when we try to reduce our options to the minimum, the other options do not disappear: we are only choosing to simplify something.\n\nWith this in mind, we must keep learning how to share with more than two options -- to understand not only ourselves, but all the other roads life puts in front of us.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
